# What the clipboard held on its way to the child, which is never what the
# child hears: the sanitizing is inside the paste, and `writes` below is the
# whole of what came out.
#
# A plain run with nobody having asked for the wrapping. Nothing to take out
# and nothing to put round it, so it arrives as itself.
paste "echo hello"
# The same with the child having asked for bracketed paste, which is what a
# shell with a line editor does. The two sequences round it are the engine's.
out "\x1b[?2004h"
paste "echo hello"
# Newlines, which are the reason for the warning: inside the wrapping they are
# text, and the shell holds them rather than running them.
paste "one\x0atwo\x0athree"
# The dangerous control bytes — NUL, ESC and DEL — which become spaces. This
# is the paste a user made after reading the warning and going ahead: there is
# no second call that would have kept them.
paste "a\x00b\x1bc\x7fd"
# The end sequence inside the content, which without the sanitizing would
# close the wrapping early and leave the rest being read as commands. The
# escape goes first, so what is left is text and the one closing is still at
# the end.
paste "a\x1b[201~rm -rf /"
# And the same run with the wrapping turned off, where the newlines become the
# carriage returns a person would have typed. Nothing can make that safe, which
# is what the warning is for.
out "\x1b[?2004l"
paste "one\x0atwo"
